The reason for the previous timing differences is unresolved. This stage separates confirmed source changes from descriptive variability and prepares one narrow wrapper hypothesis. It performs no timed comparative study, memory measurement, or profiling, and establishes no speed or acceptance claim.

The latest measured candidate did not keep the complete all-percentile wrapper unchanged. Its standard arithmetic body is exact, and its percentile collection plus numeric stable-sort operations retain the old semantics, but reduceField's multi-selection loop changed from Object.assign(result, special(...)) to direct result assignment with imported preparePercentileValues. These are concrete wrapper/function-layout changes, not an arithmetic change. The old direct single-selection branch and registry closures also dispatch to the imported helper outside special. Even when a standard request skips the special loop body, it executes a differently shaped reduceField function. Static inspection cannot establish resulting compiler or inlining decisions.

The same fixed baseline is slower between the preceding and latest windows: small-standard median +20.3875%, large-standard +18.8847%, all-percentiles +4.2862%, and all-calculations +7.3784%. Original medians also rose (+30.2608%, +5.4801%, +7.2041%, +5.9260%, respectively). These are descriptive comparisons across different windows, not a controlled new study and not a correction factor. They support retaining machine/runtime variation as a live hypothesis. Uncontrolled Windows/user activity, scheduler effects, process startup and V8 state remain possible. No source analysis can identify their contribution.

Latest all-percentiles has a +37.9967% ratio of candidate/baseline medians, versus +6.0910% median per-repetition percentage; five of seven candidate repetitions are slower, and observed ranges overlap. The baseline range width is 79.4049% of its median, versus candidate 42.8759%. Small-standard widths are 47.0562% and 49.5722%; all-calculation widths are 35.1014% and 14.6270%. These are descriptive ranges, not confidence intervals or proof of a systematic wrapper cost. All-percentiles and all-calculations use just two warmup calls and three measured calls in the fixed historical protocol. That protocol has not been changed, and those counts do not establish stabilized compiler behavior. CPU values reused from the records include child process threads and cannot isolate GC, compilation, contention or wall-clock delays.

The new candidate is produced from exact baseline9269...91371 by two reversible text additions: one import of singlePercentile from the unchanged pinned algorithm helper, and one shared-absent dispatch within special's existing percentile branch. It restores baseline doStandardCalcs, registry, reduceField and transport bodies byte-identically. A single percentile request and direct registry percentile invocation reach the selector; cached requests return before dispatch; multiple-percentile, mixed and duplicate selections retain the baseline shared sort and Object.assign behavior. The extra shared check inside special may itself add overhead. This is an explicit hypothesis, not a promised improvement.

The algorithm helper remains de334c...bd58c unchanged, including work budgets, deterministic pivots, signed-zero/coercion refusal and stable-sort fallback. No pivot, work cap, special result arithmetic, cache merge, unknown-ID handling or null-mode behavior is tuned. The original finite/sparse/coercion scope remains explicit: ordinary field/state/config objects and primitive arrays; arbitrary proxies/getters, classes, custom registry mutation and original full-product behavior are outside the finite contract.

Preflight checks authenticate the original six actual CJS files and actual Lodash archive, then compare full fresh/cache/state outputs and direct registry calls. Eight correctness-only dispatch probes verify which paths reach the helper, with an import substitution confined to a separate unmeasured probe module. The full 2,910,751 reducer /14,240 helper /48,240 cache campaign is a regenerated replay of the existing356-input seeded campaign; it is not a newly independent corpus. The raw inherited driver receipt's historical 'priorStudyCorpusReused:false' description does not redefine that fact for this stage. Its existing bounded25,000-sample/124-selection elapsed diagnostic is preserved as a correctness-limit diagnostic; it is not a new timed comparative study. Held-out source-shaped selection, coercion, null, sparse, sentinel, cache and caller-mutation checks are also rerun. All raw attempts and failures remain retained.

Before any future measurement, root must authorize a newly frozen harness and each fresh quiet window. The preferred diagnostic is a same-window comparison of exact baseline, latest measured algorithm wrapper, and the new wrapper candidate, with the authentic original as a separately identified fourth role. The fixed seven input identities, seven repetitions and original two-warmup/three-iteration multi-selection protocol stay mandatory. Four roles would mean196 sequential fresh timing children; conservatively reserve8–12 minutes. No controller or measured branch is ready or executed in this stage. A new readiness task must freeze imports, loaded-role count, order, process controls, fixture identities, source captures and prospective failure/censoring rules; changing the loaded-role count from the prior three must be disclosed as a new diagnostic design, not silently equated with the old study.

Only after that faithful diagnostic is frozen, separate supplementary controls could test a baseline-body clone with an inert helper import, direct doStandardCalcs calls versus wrapper calls, and explicitly increased warmup/iteration counts for the existing all-percentile input. They must receive distinct names, deterministic inputs and prospective counts; they must not replace the seven fixed workloads or retrospectively select a best case. Module-layout/inlining and compiler hypotheses require explicitly authorized profiling or controlled comparative measurements; source equality alone does not prove equal generated machine code. Memory, if later requested, requires a separate fresh authorization and exactly one implementation per child, with all signed deltas, zero-control unavailability and observer identity limits retained.

Production, the public preserved studies, older private studies, all fixed evaluation inputs and every frozen threshold remain unchanged. No candidate is accepted or integrated. Universal correctness, full-product completeness, full parity, performance acceptance and superiority remain false.
